    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Weather Report</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Today's Weather Will Be Sunny with Some Rain Later.</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Present findings from a simple survey supported with visual material.<br>interactive: Discuss plans for a trip.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present weather survey results with visual support (e.g., 'Most students like sunny days for outdoor activities.').<br>Can ask and clarify directions for outdoor activities (e.g., 'How is the weather today for going outside?').<br>Can discuss trip plans with classmates, based on the weather report (e.g., 'Let's visit the park when it is sunny.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and asks, "What's the weather like today?" The teacher uses a simple weather chart with icons (sun, cloud, rain, wind) to elicit responses. Students say 1-2 words (e.g., "Sunny!", "Windy!"). Teacher shows pictures of different weather conditions (sunny, rainy, windy, cloudy) and reviews the vocabulary introduced in previous lessons. Teacher asks, "What season is it now?" to connect to the theme.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a sunny day and says, "It will be sunny tomorrow." The teacher repeats the sentence, emphasizing the intonation on "sunny." The teacher then shows a picture of rain and says, "It looks like rain today; you should bring an umbrella." The teacher explicitly models the future simple tense and modal verb 'should'. For phonemic awareness, the teacher segments a multisyllabic word: "ther-mo-stat" by clapping each syllable, encouraging students to repeat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a cloudy sky: "Will it be sunny?" (Expected answer: "No.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to an umbrella: "If it looks like rain, what should I bring?" (Expected answer: "An umbrella.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, animated weather report video (1-2 minutes) or a visual infographic depicting a 3-day forecast, focusing on the future simple tense for predictions and recommendations. The report includes weather conditions (sunny, cloudy, rainy, windy) and temperature (warm, cold, hot), along with some prepositions of place (e.g., "The rain is in the east").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic competence – Describing weather conditions: 'It will be sunny tomorrow.' Asking about the forecast: 'What's the temperature tomorrow?' Using prepositions of place: 'The rain is in the east.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> After watching/viewing, the teacher asks, "What is the report mostly about: animals or the weather?" (Expected answer: "The weather.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Teacher states sentences based on the report. Students say "True" or "False."
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Tomorrow, it will be cold and rainy." (False, if it's sunny)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The temperature will be warm in the afternoon." (True/False based on report)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Teacher provides two columns: Days (e.g., "Monday," "Tuesday") and Weather icons. Students match the day to the correct weather icon shown in the report.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice speaking using the future simple tense for weather predictions, prepositions of place, and modal verbs for recommendations. Target vocabulary: weather, forecast, report, sun, rain, wind, cloud, temperature, will be, look like, bring, sunny, cloudy, rainy, windy, warm, cold, on, at, in.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion (Individual/Choral):</b> The teacher displays incomplete sentences on the board or flashcards. Students complete them orally, focusing on the target grammar and vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Tomorrow, it _____ (be) sunny." (Students say: "will be")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If it looks like rain, you _____ (should) bring an umbrella." (Students say: "should")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The storm is _____ (over/in) the lake." (Students say: "over" or "in")</li>
                        </ul>
                        Teacher provides immediate feedback on pronunciation and grammar.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Prediction & Recommendation Chains (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. Student A starts by giving a simple weather prediction for a day (e.g., "Tomorrow, it will be windy."). Student B then makes a recommendation based on that prediction using "you should" and includes a place or time (e.g., "You should bring a jacket. We will stay in the house."). Then Student B makes a prediction for another day, and Student A responds. The teacher circulates, monitoring for correct use of grammar and vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Pair Interaction: "Ask the Forecast" (Pair Work):</b> Students are given simple weather maps for two different imaginary cities with 2-day forecasts. In pairs, Student A asks Student B about the forecast for City X (e.g., "What will the weather be like in City X on Friday?"). Student B answers using the map (e.g., "It will be cloudy and warm. The temperature will be 25 degrees."). They then switch roles for City Y. Teacher encourages using vocabulary like 'forecast,' 'prediction,' 'temperature,' and practicing intonation for questions and statements.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Role-Play and Mini-Presentation: "Planning Our Weekend Trip."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a simple visual weather report for a fictional upcoming weekend (e.g., two days, different weather for each). The report includes a simple graph or chart from a "Class Weather Survey" (e.g., "Most students like sunny days for the beach (80%), some like cloudy days for hiking (15%).").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their task is to discuss and decide on plans for a weekend trip (e.g., to the beach, mountains, park) based on the weather forecast and their "survey findings."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student asks about the forecast: "What will the weather be like on Saturday, according to the report?" The other student presents the weather findings for that day: "On Saturday, it will be sunny and warm." They then discuss: "We should go to the beach because most students like sunny days for the beach." They can also say, "The breeze will be nice at the beach."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must use: future simple (will be), modal verbs (should), prepositions of place (e.g., 'The clouds are over the mountains'), and the target vocabulary (sunny, warm, cold, rainy, forecast, temperature, bring, sky, etc.). They practice intonation in their predictions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, each pair briefly presents their chosen trip plan to another pair or the class (e.g., "Our plan for Saturday is to go to the beach because it will be sunny. We should bring sunscreen!"). They use their survey results to support their decision.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Weekend Trip Plan" to the class or another pair, demonstrating their ability to discuss trip plans based on a weather report and survey results.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can present weather survey results with visual support.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can ask and clarify questions about the weather forecast.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can discuss trip plans collaboratively with classmates.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses future simple tense (will be) for predictions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses modal verbs (should) for recommendations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target weather vocabulary (e.g., sunny, rainy, warm, forecast, temperature).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation and intonation in predictions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts on a small piece of paper before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new weather word or phrase I used today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Something I found easy/challenging when talking about the weather was..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (oral or written):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now discussing weather and making plans?" (Thumbs up/down or 1-5 scale)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's lesson helped me the most to speak about the weather?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking-focused lesson? (e.g., students effectively used future simple, enjoyed the role-play, peer discussions were lively).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to further improve their speaking accuracy and fluency? (e.g., more practice with specific prepositions of place, clearer visual cues for survey results, more direct pronunciation drills for tricky words).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition):</b> Today, we talked a lot about the weather and our trip plans. Next lesson, we will write our own short weather reports and describe our ideal trip based on the forecast.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>